  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1146 PM CDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nAn upper level low continued to spin along the MN/IA border \nthis evening, with a shortwave very evident in water vapor \nimagery edging southeast through western SD. Clouds and low \nlevel moisture will linger in much of the area overnight into \nTuesday and as the aforementioned shortwave pushes in early \nTuesday morning, could see a little drizzle develop (20% \nchance). A few spotty showers will also be possible in the \nafternoon/evening as some additional shortwave energy currently \nover UT moves through. In general, though, expect more dry time \nthan wet on Tuesday with temperatures holding in the 60s.\n\nHeading into Wednesday, southeasterly surface flow will ramp up\nwith warm air advection helping to lead to showers and perhaps \na few storms, mostly to the west of the forecast area, but a few\ncould sneak in during the day. Despite the southerly flow, \ntemperatures aren't expected to warm all that much, with cloud \ncover still lingering. If you manage to stay dry on Wednesday, \nthat'll be the day you'll want to mow the lawn, because by \nWednesday night into Thursday, we'll see a steady stream of \nshortwave energy coming out of the Desert Southwest combined \nwith persistent warm air advection and moisture transport \npointing into the area. As a result, we'll see rain chances \nlargely remaining above 30-40% through Saturday, generally \npeaking in the 70-90% range during the overnight and morning \nhours each day. With very little instability, not expecting much\nin the way of thunderstorms, but there could be a rumble of \nthunder every now and then.\n\nSunday is trending toward a dry and warmer day as an upper \nlevel ridge axis quickly slides through and temperatures get \ninto the mid 70s to around 80. However, the dry period looks \nshort-lived with guidance in good agreement that a surface \nboundary slides through sometime Monday or Tuesday and gives us \nadditional widespread rain.",
